Add: readable per-request arrival/completion logging (NEW REQUEST /
FINISHED), replacing the noisy per-engine-step "generation [...] | GPU KV
cache usage: ...%" line (--enable-logging-iteration-details) as the
day-to-day way to watch a live server.

Motivation: --enable-logging-iteration-details (ITERATION_LOG=1 in
micke-start.sh) logs one line per non-dummy engine step. Under MTP
speculative decoding this deployment runs, that's nearly every decode
step of every live generation -- e.g.
"Engine 000: generation [chatcmpl-...] (1 req, 4 verified, 1 accepted) =
81.2 tok/s | GPU KV cache usage: 14.7%" firing several times a second per
active conversation. Useful for step-by-step debugging, but too frequent
to actually read live, and it answers "what is the engine doing right
now" rather than what you actually want while watching a server: did
this request show up with a warm cache, and how did it do end to end?
micke-start.sh's ITERATION_LOG default flipped to 0 in the same change
that adds this (per-request lines below cover the everyday use case; the
per-step detail is still available via ITERATION_LOG=1 for debugging).

New module: v1/engine/request_lifecycle_log.py, following the same
pattern as the existing v1/kv_offload/transfer_log.py (readable per-
request KV block transfer logging from an earlier session of this
deployment's investigation): plain INFO logger.info() calls, gated by an
env var read once at import (VLLM_REQUEST_LIFECYCLE_LOG, default on,
matching VLLM_KV_TRANSFER_LOG's convention), request ids truncated the
same way (_REQ_ID_MAX=34, matching what appears in request log filenames
and the API response) so all of KV LOAD/STORE, generation, NEW REQUEST
and FINISHED lines can be grepped together by the same id prefix.

Two call sites, both in v1/engine/output_processor.py (no new fields
needed -- every value used was already being computed and stored on
RequestState/RequestStateStats for Prometheus, just never surfaced as a
readable line):

- NEW REQUEST, in process_outputs()'s existing `if req_state.is_prefilling
  and engine_core_output.prefill_stats is not None:` block -- this is the
  earliest point a request's cache-hit outcome is actually known (not
  admission time: the cached fraction isn't determined until the first
  prefill chunk runs). Uses req_state.prompt_len and the
  num_cached_tokens just captured onto req_state on the very same lines.
  Fires exactly once per request (guarded by is_prefilling's one-way
  True->False transition, same guard the existing code already relies
  on).

- FINISHED, in _update_stats_from_finished() -- fires exactly once per
  request, when finish_reason is not None. Reuses
  req_state.stats (RequestStateStats): first_token_ts, scheduled_ts,
  last_token_ts and first_token_latency are already populated by
  IterationStats.update_from_output() by the time this runs (same fields
  vLLM's own update_from_finished_request() uses two lines below, for
  the Prometheus FinishedRequestStats path) -- prefill_time =
  first_token_ts - scheduled_ts, decode_time = last_token_ts -
  first_token_ts, matching that existing computation exactly. PP token
  count is req_state.prompt_len - req_state.num_cached_tokens (the
  PrefillStats invariant computed - prompt - cached already guarantees
  this equals num_computed_tokens, so no new field needed on
  RequestState). An aborted/errored request that never reached
  scheduling has all these timestamps at their 0.0 default; the format
  functions render "?" for a rate instead of dividing by a zero or
  negative interval, so this never raises or prints a nonsense rate.

Sample output:

    NEW REQUEST chatcmpl-b746f047edffef5e-9a1f8167: 83746 prompt (97.5% cached, 742 uncached tokens)
